.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The connection protocol (RFC 4254): channels multiplexed over one authenticated transport, with their flow control, requests and TCP forwarding.
.PP
A gf_chans object is the same on both ends: either side opens channels and accepts them.  Like the authentication machines it attaches to the transport through a send callback and an input call for the messages numbered 80 to 100; it does no I/O and holds no clocks.
.PP
Flow control is strict, and it is the application's.  The peer may send only as much as the window we gave it; more is a protocol error and ends the connection.  The window is given back as the application says it has dealt with the data (gf_chans_consumed), not as it arrives, so a slow consumer slows the sender instead of filling memory.  Nothing is buffered for sending either: gf_chans_send_data takes what the peer's window and packet size allow and returns how much that was; when it returns less than asked the writable event says when to try again.
.PP
What it checks, and ends the connection for: data, requests or window changes on a channel that is not open; data after EOF or past the window or the packet size; a window beyond 2^32 \- 1; a reply nobody is waiting for; more pending requests or channels than the configured limits; and every malformed message.
.PP
What it refuses by itself, replying failure: X11 forwarding and agent forwarding (both extend the trust of the connection to other credentials of a machine), channel types other than session, direct\-tcpip, forwarded\-tcpip and OpenSSH's Unix socket counterparts (direct\-streamlocal and forwarded\-streamlocal), and requests and global requests it does not know, which is also how the "keepalive@openssh.com" request is answered.
.PP
Channel numbers are the local ones: gf_chans allocates them and the application names channels by them.  They are reused only after a channel is closed on both sides.
