.br
long \fBgf_chans_send_extended\fP (gf_chans *m, uint32_t id, uint32_t type, const uint8_t *data, size_t len);
Send up to LEN bytes; returns how many were taken (possibly 0), or a negative status.  Offer everything you have rather than first checking gf_chans_writable: a result below LEN is what arms the writable event. GF_ERR_AGAIN means the transport could not take it now (a key exchange is full); try again after gf_chans_resume.
.TP
int \fBgf_chans_consumed\fP (gf_chans *m, uint32_t id, size_t n);
Tell the library N bytes of received data were dealt with, so that the peer may send that much more.
.TP
int \fBgf_chans_send_eof\fP (gf_chans *m, uint32_t id);
No more data from us.
.TP
int \fBgf_chans_close\fP (gf_chans *m, uint32_t id);
Close.  The channel is gone when the peer has closed too.
.TP
int \fBgf_chans_resume\fP (gf_chans *m);
Call this from the transport's rekeyed event: every channel whose send was refused with GF_ERR_AGAIN, or cut short, while the transport held messages back gets its writable event, if the peer's window allows.
